  "strategy": "**Approach and Rationale:**  \nWe are minimizing the evaluation function \\( \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max(\\text{conv})}{(\\sum a)^2} \\). The key insight is that sequences with plateaus and valleys (i.e., multiple segments of positive coefficients separated by zeros) can reduce the maximum value in their self-convolution compared to uniform or random sequences. This structured approach helps escape local minima while maintaining a high attack surface.\n\n1. **Initial Candidate**: Start with the provided parent sequence (if available) and normalize it.  \n2. **Plateau and Valley Parametrization**: For a given sequence length \\( n \\), generate candidates by defining two intervals:  \n   - **Plateau Top**: Range \\([start, end]\\) where coefficients are positive.  \n   - **Valley Bottom**: Range \\([start - r1, end - r2]\\) where coefficients are zeros (or negative if needed, but normalized to non-negative).  \n3. **Fourier Optimization**: Use Fourier coefficients to approximate and refine candidate sequences, targeting a low maximum convolution value.  \n\nThe algorithm focuses on sequences with tailored plateau heights and strategic gaps to minimize the impact of overlapping terms in the convolution. Differences from the previous program:  \n- **Variable Structure**: Instead of fixed two-plateau lengths, we use plateau heights and gap controls.  \n- **Gradient-Free Refinement**: We rely on Fourier-based heuristics for local optimization.  \n\n**Hypothesis**: This structure will yield sequences with lower max convolution values.  \n\n---\n\n**Mathematical Specification**  \nWe represent a candidate sequence as:  \n\\[  \na_i = \\begin{cases}  \n\\frac{h}{\\sum a} & \\text{if } i \\in [start, end] \\\\  \n0 & \\text{otherwise}  \n\\end{cases}  \n\\]  \nwhere:  \n- \\( n \\) is the sequence length.  \n- \\( h \\) is the height of the plateau (scaled to ensure sum=1 after normalization).  \n\nThe evaluation function becomes:  \n\\[  \n\\text{score} = \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max(\\text{conv}_{\\text{full}}(a, a))}{(\\sum a)^2}  \n\\]  \nHealth check:  \n- Ensure \\( \\sum a \\geq 0.01 \\).  \n- Normalize all coefficients to non-negative and scale to 0-1000.  \n\n**Variables**:  \n- \\( n \\): Length of sequence (integer).  \n- \\( start, end \\): Plateau start and end indices.  \n- \\( gap \\): Interval of zeros controlling valley depth.  \n\n**Objective** (after normalization):  \n\\[  \n\\min \\left( \\frac{2n \\cdot \\max(\\text{conv}_{\\text{full}}(a, a))}{(\\sum a)^2} \\right)  \n\\]  \nWe use Fourier coefficients as a surrogate:  \n\\[  \n\\text{Fourier}(a) = \\sum_{k=0}^{M} c_k \\cdot \\cos\\left(\\frac{2\\pi k i}{n}\\right) + s_k \\cdot \\sin\\left(\\frac{2\\pi k i}{n}\\right) \\]  \nwith parameters \\( M \\) (degree) and \\( (c_k, s_k) \\).  \n\n---\n\n**Implementation Procedure**  \n1. **Initialization**:  \n   - If the parent sequence exists, load it and re-initialize it with improved normalization.  \n   - If not, start with a simple plateau sequence.  \n\n2. **Candidate Generation**:  \n   - Define helper functions for generating sequences, padding with zeros if necessary, and converting between sequence and Fourier representations.  \n   - Use random plateau intervals \\([start, end]\\) and gap values to form candidates.  \n\n3. **Fourier Optimization** (Phase 1):  \n   - Start with uniform candidates derived from the plateau structure.  \n   - Use a low-dimensional optimization (e.g., Nelder-Mead) on the Fourier coefficients.  \n   - Project results to non-negative simplex and re-normalize.  \n\n4. **Fourier Refinement** (Phase 2):  \n   - Increase max degree \\( M \\) in the Fourier representation to capture higher-frequency details.  \n   - Heuristic refinement by adjusting plateau heights based on kernel operations.  \n\n5. **Post-Processing**:  \n   - Verify the sequence by passing it to \\( \\text{evaluate\\_sequence} \\).  \n   - Compare the result with the global minimum score.  \n   - Maintain a \"best\" candidate through all optimization cycles.  \n\n**Algorithm Outline**:  \n1. Load initial sequence from parents or generate a default.  \n2. While within time:  \n   a. Random candidate positions.  \n   b. Fourier-parameterize the candidate (scale coefficients).  \n   c. Use optimization to update parameters.  \n   d. Verify against \\( \\text{evaluate\\_sequence} \\) and update best if applicable.  \n\n---\n\n**Search Choices**  \nVariable configurations:  \n- **Time Budget**: 1800 seconds.  \n- **Sequence Length**: 128-2048. Start with a wide plateau modulus.  \n- **Fourier Degree**: \\( M \\) from 1 to 50 (increase with iterations).  \n- **Plateau Spring Size**: High curvature edges up to length \\( n/3 \\).  \n- **Gap Multiples**: 0 useful gaps in convolution.  \n\n`FourierDegree` = 12 initially, then 20 for deeper optimization.  \n\n---\n\n**Compute Budget**  \n- **Convolution Cost**: For a sequence of length \\( n \\) is approximately \\( O(n \\log n) \\) using FFT.  \n- **Fourier Optimization Cost**: Each iteration reduces the search space by an order of \\( n \\).  \n- **Breakout if No Progress**: Use a alpha-min tolerance or a time-controlled early termination.  \n\n**Example**:  \nFor n=384, plateau degree 20 om code that has mandatory re-normalization.\n\n---\n\n**Validation and Return Contract**  \nFinally, all candidates are converted to valid sequences and evaluated. The best score is kept, unless the parent improves it. The returned sequence must:  \n- Be a list of positive or zero floats (no +inf or NaN).  \n- Reside within the given length order and be padded to zero if missing.  \n\nCompare with `evaluate_sequence` using the actual function to ensure:  \n\\[  \n\\frac{2n \\cdot \\max(\\text{conv}_{\\text{full}}(a, a))}{(\\sum a)^2} < \\text{best score}  \n\\]",
